    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the feedback loop? Like, how does the reduction in angiotensin II affect the release of renin and aldosterone specifically? I want to make sure I have the whole chain down for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors primarily interfere with the RAAS pathway by blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. In a normal feedback loop, angiotensin II has a few key roles. It constricts blood vessels to increase blood pressure, stimulates aldosterone release which promotes sodium and water retention, and inhibits renin release through negative feedback. When ACE inhibitors block the formation of angiotensin II, the initial constriction of blood vessels diminishes. With restricted angiotensin II levels, aldosterone secretion is blunted, leading to decreased sodium and water retention, which in turn lowers blood pressure. As for renin release, since angiotensin II is also responsible for inhibiting renin secretion, lower levels release by the unaffected angiotensin I trigger one last twist in the feedback loop: sympathetic nervous system activation. This occurs as the body attempts to compensate for the lowered blood pressure by promoting vasoconstriction and an increased heart rate, thus completing the opposing phases of the feedback mechanism, which can be challenging for the body to achieve completely.",
      "source": "target",
