    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, I think I follow the feedback loop now. One thing that's been tripping me up in practice questions is the side effects, specifically the dry cough. Why does inhibiting the ACE enzyme cause that? What's the biochemical connection there?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The persistent dry cough associated with ACE inhibitors like lisinopril is directly related to the enzyme’s interaction with bradykinin, a peptide that elevates with the reduced breakdown due to ACE inhibition. Bradykinin is known to dilate blood vessels, stimulate pain nerve fibers that signal pain, and induce the production of substances like prostaglandins and nitric oxide that cause inflammation. In the respiratory tract, excess bradykinin can lead to inflammation of the airways, manifesting as a dry cough. Not all individuals experience this side effect, but recognizing its potential is important when considering patient management and treatment alternatives. Adjustments in medication or a switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB) could be potential solutions for patients who are unable to continue their ACE inhibitor therapy due to this side effect.",
